diagnosis_text:
The fabric is healthy; no device is currently failed. End-to-end testing shows pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) can reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT in both directions (topology-wide reachability check). The “big middle box” spine_router_0_0 is up and routing: its FRR daemon is active (systemd reports frr.service active/running), its BGP sessions to the super spine (172.16.0.0) and both leaves (172.16.0.5/65200 and 172.16.0.7/65201) are Established with prefixes received, and its kernel routing table contains the learned /24 networks for both racks via the appropriate leaf-facing interfaces. The super spine and the other spine also have all BGP neighbors up.

Mechanism: In this CLOS design, traffic between racks ECMPs across the two spines. If one spine were actually down, traffic should fail over via the remaining spine after a brief convergence; it would not remain black-holed. Current evidence shows no loss and all control-plane sessions stable.

Localization: Verified directly on spine_router_0_0 (interfaces eth0-2 up; BGP neighbors up ~2 minutes) and via successful host-to-host pings.

Root cause: No present outage; the reported failure was likely a transient flap that has since recovered or a misinterpretation.

Corrective fix: None required. Do not reboot spine_router_0_0. Recommend checking FRR logs/telemetry for any brief overnight neighbor flaps and keeping monitoring alerts tuned for transient vs persistent failures.
